    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Does blocking AI crawlers hurt my Google Search SEO rankings?
          <span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          No, blocking AI training crawlers (GPTBot, ClaudeBot, CCBot, and Google-Extended) has zero impact on your traditional Google Search rankings. Google Search relies on Googlebot, which operates independently from the Google-Extended training token.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Do all AI bots respect robots.txt Disallow rules?
          <span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          No, robots.txt is a voluntary instruction. Reputable operators like OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and Apple honor robots.txt for their training crawlers, whereas aggressive scrapers and spoofed bots ignore it unless you block their User-Agent and IP ranges with a Cloudflare WAF or NGINX HTTP 403 rule.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          How do I block AI from training on my content while still appearing in ChatGPT Search and Perplexity?
          <span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          In practice, we recommend a split robots.txt policy: set Disallow: / for training crawlers (GPTBot, ClaudeBot, Google-Extended, Applebot-Extended, CCBot, Meta-ExternalAgent) while setting Allow: / for search indexers (OAI-SearchBot and PerplexityBot). This keeps your brand cited with clickable links in AI answers while opting out of model training.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Why are AI bots still hitting my server after I blocked them in robots.txt?
          <span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          In real-world server log audits, we see two culprits: first, CDN caching serving an old copy of your robots.txt file (purge your CDN cache after editing robots.txt), and second, non-compliant scrapers or user-triggered fetchers that ignore robots.txt and require a hard WAF or NGINX 403 block.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
